<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Az-Zahir Ghazi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1172–1216</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as al-Zahir Ghazi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Az-Zahir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a> was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> and Ayyubid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> from 1186 to 1216. He kept his principality independent of his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adil">al-Adil</a>, strengthened Aleppo's citadel and built mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, making the city a centre of learning. His descendants ruled Aleppo until the Mongol conquest of 1260.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/az-zahir-ghazi.jpg" alt="Az-Zahir Ghazi" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayyubid_Az_Zahir_1204_Aleppo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
